# The Pleiades with Gaia

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:space_tap, "~> 0.3.0"},
  {:explorer, "~> 0.13"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19.1"},
  {:kino_explorer, "~> 0.1.25"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1.13"}
])
```

## The cluster

The Pleiades (M45) is an open cluster: a few thousand stars born together about 100 million years ago, some 135 parsecs away. [Gaia](https://www.cosmos.esa.int/web/gaia) measures the position, parallax, proper motion and colour of nearly two billion stars. Its archive at ESA is a TAP service, and so is the GAVO Data Center, the German Virtual Observatory's archive, which holds a copy of Gaia DR3 in `gaia.dr3lite`.

This notebook asks GAVO for the stars around the cluster, picks out the members by how they move, and draws their colour–magnitude diagram, the observer's version of the Hertzsprung–Russell diagram.

## The query

A cone of 2° around the centre of the cluster. The parallax filter keeps stars between 100 and 200 parsecs away (parallaxes of 10 to 5 milliarcseconds), which drops most of the stars in front of and behind the cluster. `bp_rp` is the colour, the difference between Gaia's blue and red magnitudes, and the diagram needs it. `gaia.dr3lite` has the two magnitudes but not their difference, so the query computes it.

`format: :votable` asks GAVO for VOTable instead of CSV, so each value comes with the type its column declares: numbers to compute with, not strings to parse.

```elixir
require Explorer.DataFrame, as: DF
alias Explorer.Series
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

gavo = "https://dc.g-vo.org/tap"

adql = """
SELECT source_id, ra, dec, parallax, pmra, pmdec, phot_g_mean_mag,
  phot_bp_mean_mag - phot_rp_mean_mag AS bp_rp
FROM gaia.dr3lite
WHERE CONTAINS(POINT('ICRS', ra, dec), CIRCLE('ICRS', 56.75, 24.12, 2.0)) = 1
  AND parallax BETWEEN 5 AND 10
  AND phot_bp_mean_mag IS NOT NULL
  AND phot_rp_mean_mag IS NOT NULL
"""

{:ok, result} =
  SpaceTAP.query(gavo, adql, format: :votable, req_options: [receive_timeout: 60_000])

%{stars: length(result.rows), overflow: result.overflow}
```

`overflow` is `false`, so GAVO sent every star that matches: it didn't cut the result at a limit of its own (DALI 1.1 §4.4.1). A CSV result couldn't say.

The VOTable describes each column too, with the unit and the UCD, a standard label for what the column measures:

```elixir
result.fields
|> Enum.map(&Map.take(&1, [:name, :datatype, :unit, :ucd, :description]))
|> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: [:name, :datatype, :unit, :ucd, :description], name: "Columns")
```

The result becomes a data frame, an [Explorer](https://hexdocs.pm/explorer/) table that keeps each column as one array, and which Livebook shows as a table you can sort and filter:

```elixir
stars = SpaceTAP.Result.to_dataframe(result)
```

Each column has the type of its field: `source_id` is a `long`, so it becomes `s64`, a 64-bit integer, `ra` and `dec` are `double`s, `f64`, and the rest are `float`s, `f32`, 32-bit numbers. The charts below run in JavaScript, whose numbers can't hold every integer as large as a `source_id`, so the colour–magnitude diagram gets it as a string.

## Who belongs to the cluster

Stars in a cluster move through space together, so they share a proper motion, the motion across the sky in milliarcseconds per year. Field stars at the same distance move every which way. In a plot of proper motions the cluster is the tight clump:

```elixir
Vl.new(width: 500, height: 400, title: "Proper motions")
|> Vl.data_from_values(stars, only: ["pmra", "pmdec"])
|> Vl.mark(:circle, size: 10, opacity: 0.5)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "pmra", type: :quantitative, title: "pmRA (mas/yr)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "pmdec", type: :quantitative, title: "pmDec (mas/yr)")
```

The clump sits near +20 mas/yr in right ascension and −45 mas/yr in declination. Take as members the stars within 5 mas/yr of that point. `DF.mutate/2` adds a column computed from the others, a whole column at a time, and `^` brings in a value from outside the data frame:

```elixir
{pmra_cluster, pmdec_cluster} = {20.0, -45.5}

stars =
  DF.mutate(stars,
    member: ((pmra - ^pmra_cluster) ** 2 + (pmdec - ^pmdec_cluster) ** 2) ** 0.5 < 5
  )

members = DF.filter(stars, member)

%{stars: DF.n_rows(stars), members: DF.n_rows(members)}
```

```elixir
Vl.new(width: 500, height: 400, title: "Proper motions, members in colour")
|> Vl.data_from_values(stars, only: ["pmra", "pmdec", "member"])
|> Vl.mark(:circle, size: 10, opacity: 0.5)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "pmra", type: :quantitative, title: "pmRA (mas/yr)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "pmdec", type: :quantitative, title: "pmDec (mas/yr)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "member", type: :nominal, title: "Member")
```

## The colour–magnitude diagram

The apparent magnitude depends on distance. The absolute magnitude, how bright a star would look from 10 parsecs, comes from the parallax ϖ in milliarcseconds:

$$
M_G = G + 5 \log_{10}(\varpi) - 10
$$

```elixir
members =
  DF.mutate(members,
    abs_g: phot_g_mean_mag + 5 * log(parallax, 10) - 10,
    id: cast(source_id, :string)
  )

Vl.new(width: 500, height: 500, title: "The Pleiades: colour–magnitude diagram")
|> Vl.data_from_values(members, only: ["bp_rp", "abs_g", "id"])
|> Vl.mark(:circle, size: 12, opacity: 0.7)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "bp_rp", type: :quantitative, title: "BP − RP (colour)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "abs_g", type: :quantitative, title: "M_G", scale: [reverse: true])
|> Vl.encode_field(:tooltip, "id", type: :nominal, title: "source_id")
```

Bright stars are at the top, since magnitudes grow as stars get fainter, and blue stars are on the left. Almost every member lies on one diagonal band, the main sequence: stars that burn hydrogen in their cores. The cluster is young, so even its hot, blue, massive stars at the top left are still on it. The faint red end at the bottom right is made of low-mass red dwarfs.

## The cluster on the sky

Right ascension grows to the east, which is to the left on a map of the sky, so the x axis is reversed. Brighter stars are drawn bigger:

```elixir
members_on_sky = DF.mutate(members, brightness: 20 - phot_g_mean_mag)

Vl.new(width: 500, height: 500, title: "Members on the sky")
|> Vl.data_from_values(members_on_sky, only: ["ra", "dec", "brightness"])
|> Vl.mark(:circle, color: "#4c78a8", opacity: 0.6)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "ra",
  type: :quantitative,
  title: "RA (°)",
  scale: [reverse: true, zero: false]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "dec", type: :quantitative, title: "Dec (°)", scale: [zero: false])
|> Vl.encode_field(:size, "brightness",
  type: :quantitative,
  legend: nil,
  scale: [range: [2, 200]]
)
```

The big dots near the centre are the stars you can see with the naked eye, such as Atlas, Electra, Maia and Taygeta. Two are missing. Alcyone, the brightest, has no parallax in Gaia DR3: the value is null, so `parallax BETWEEN 5 AND 10` drops it. Merope is in the result, but its proper motion is about 7 mas/yr from the cluster's, outside the 5 mas/yr circle. Very bright stars saturate Gaia's detectors, so their measurements are less precise.

## How far away

The distance in parsecs is 1000 divided by the parallax in milliarcseconds. The mean parallax of the members gives the distance to the cluster:

```elixir
mean_parallax = Series.mean(members["parallax"])

%{mean_parallax_mas: Float.round(mean_parallax, 3), distance_pc: round(1000 / mean_parallax)}
```

Inverting a single noisy parallax gives a biased distance, but with hundreds of members, each with a precise parallax, the result is close to the published one, about 135 parsecs.
